
Visual Assistant Shell COMPILE
 and history notes

Notes:
  *) manual ./COMPILE.ru is totally obsoleted
  *) now build requires standard libraries
  *) low-level terminal support functions can be found in one of:
	termcap, tinfo, ncurses
  *) make sure '-devel' version installed
  *) static linkage allowed but not required as option (--static) 

Compile vash-2.0.24 and later

1.
unpack tarball and change directory on it:
        tar xzf ./vash-*.tar.gz
        cd vash-*
2.
run ./configure script or run 'make config':
        ./configure
    or
        make config
3. 
check BLD.conf:
        vi ./BLD.conf
4.
compile binaries in local subdirectory:
        make clean install
NOTE: find resulting binaries under ./BLD
        
5.
How to use vash compiled locally or running for debug.
Requires ~/bin in your PATH:
      ln -s `pwd`/bin/v.debug.sh ~/bin/vd
      vd

6.
Build installable binary packages (optional).

    6.1 RPM build
    Make sure rpmbuild package installed and working.
    NOTE: In Fedora resulting package will be in ~/rpmbuild/SRPMS
    (may be vary on a different systems)
	./MK-srpm.sh

    then find resulting source package and run rpmbuild:
	rpmbuild --rebuild ~/rpmbuild/SRPMS/vash-*.src.rpm

    find and install a package, in Fedora:
	sudo yum install ~/rpmbuild/RPMS/*/vash-*.rpm

    6.2. DEB build
    Make sure required 'dpkg' and 'chroot' packages installed.
    Run script, installable package will be in parent directory:
       ./DEBIAN.sh
       sudo dpkg -i ../vash_*.deb


HISTORICAL NOTEs

Screen  mode  interface  of  vash  was implemented with a termcap-based
linlib library. This library was thoughts inspired by some project from
CERN then used for real-time applications in 1980x.  Later, library was
extended with some tools and named visi.

Initially vash was wrote for 16-bit environment  on  DEC PDP-11/23  and
VT100  hardware,  using classic K&R dialect of C language.  Only ASCII-
based 8-bit encodings were supported including KOI8-R and ISO-8859-1.

1st stricted release was done about 1990 year under name ash, assistant
shell.  Further development was episodical.  In 1991-1993 years ash was
ported  to  i386  platforms  ISC  UNIX and FreeBSD, then UnixWare. Some
enhancements was added with port to SPARK Solaris in 1995 and DEC OSF-1
Unix in 1996. Porting to SGI IRIX was done in that time.

1st port to linux-based systems was done in 2004 since Fedora Core 2
which was selected as nearest linux for for RiP SGI O2..

In modern  time  project  development  was  reactivated  not  observing
comparable tools. Then prepared for wide publication under free license.

Finally, name VASH was assigned to this program avoiding confusion with
/bin/ash, again shell.

Most  of  previouse versions was adopted for 32-bit architectures and supported
only 8-bit encoding charsets.  Native 64-bit support added since 2019.
Probably, 16-bit version may be still compiled, I'm not sure.

Termcap-based  code  was  modified  to use terminfo.  Starting in 2020,
UTF-8 support was implemented but only tested for restricted set of  languages
including european and chinessy.

Interface of vash itself remains english only. I have no any plan to implement
any other language support. I suppose it have too little sense.

On the other hand, unicode support has already caused the code to swell
and become more complex comparing with compact and clear initial
implementation for ASCII-based 8-bit encodings in the late of 1980.

